{:edb/db-before-t 1872 :edb/db-after-t 1873 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535185 50 #inst "2026-10-08T18:59:01.610Z" 13194139535185 true] [17592186091906 1100 "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ | $13$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-12$ | $-7$ | $-3$ | $7$ | $15$ | $21$ |\n\nApproximate $f'(x)$ at the point $x=16$ using a backward difference approximation." 13194139535185 true] [17592186091906 1100 "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ | $13$ | $16$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-12$ | $-7$ | $-3$ | $7$ | $15$ | $21$ |\n\nApproximate $f'(x)$ at the point $x=16$ using a backward difference approximation." 13194139535185 false] [17592186091906 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x) - f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=16.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(16) & ≈\\frac{f(16)-f(16-3)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(16)-f(13)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{21-15}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 true] [17592186091906 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x) - f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=16$. We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=3$. This gives\n\n$$\nf^{′}(16) \\mid \\approx (f(16) - f(16 - 3))/(3); = (f(16) - f(13))/(3); = \\frac{21 - 15}{3}; = \\frac{6}{3}; = 2\n$$" 13194139535185 false] [17592186091908 1100 "A race car's position along a straight track, measured in meters from the starting line, is given by the function $s(t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ | $1.8$ | $2.0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $s(t)$ | $1.3$ | $2.7$ | $2.9$ | $3.5$ | $5.0$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity $s'(t)$ when $t=1.6$ using a backward difference approximation." 13194139535185 false] [17592186091908 1100 "A race car's position along a straight track, measured in meters from the starting line, is given by the function $s(t),$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ | $1.8$ | $2.0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $1.3$ | $2.7$ | $2.9$ | $3.5$ | $5.0$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity $s'(t)$ when $t=1.6$ using a backward difference approximation." 13194139535185 true] [17592186091908 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\ns'(t) \\approx \\frac{s(t) - s(t-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=1.6.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(1.6) & ≈\\frac{s(1.6)-s(1.6-0.2)}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(1.6)-s(1.4)}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.9-2.7}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.2}{0.2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at the time $t=1.6,$ the velocity is approximately $1\\, \\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}.$" 13194139535185 true] [17592186091908 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\ns'(t) \\approx \\frac{s(t) - s(t-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the time $t=1.6$. We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.2$. This gives\n\n$$\ns^{′}(1.6) \\mid \\approx (s(1.6) - s(1.6 - 0.2))/(0.2); = (s(1.6) - s(1.4))/(0.2); = \\frac{2.9 - 2.7}{0.2}; = \\frac{0.2}{0.2}; = 1\n$$\n\nSo at the time $t=1.6$, the velocity is approximately $1\\, \\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}$." 13194139535185 false] [17592186092031 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/6e3f77e5b7d491e3ee43c8f8150065282d2abc259e3a88751b93ff15b27f492a.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate $g'(x)$ at the point $x=6$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=4.$" 13194139535185 true] [17592186092031 1100 "The graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above.\nApproximate $g'(x)$ at the point $x=6$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=4$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/993/Source/Images/ae0cc73bdba85ba6e93ee3fd020172bd.png)" 13194139535185 false] [17592186092031 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\ng'(x) \\approx \\frac{g(x) - g(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=6$. Applying the above formula with $x=6$ and $h=4$ gives\n\n$$\ng^{′}(6) \\mid \\approx (g(6) - g(6 - 4))/(4); = (g(6) - g(2))/(4); = \\frac{6 - 4}{4}; = \\frac{2}{4}; = \\frac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139535185 false] [17592186092031 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\ng'(x) \\approx \\frac{g(x) - g(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=6.$ Applying the above formula with $x=6$ and $h=4$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{g(6)-g(6-4)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(6)-g(2)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 true] [17592186092032 1100 "A factory produces fans. The total cost of producing $x$ fans, in dollars, is given by the function $C(x)$. The graph of $C(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/993/Source/Images/5709d3ae0923e76a3e63bf4238582d36.png)\n\nApproximate the marginal cost $C'(x)$ at the point $x=3$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2$." 13194139535185 false] [17592186092032 1100 "A factory produces fans. The total cost of producing $x$ fans, in dollars, is given by the function $C(x).$  The graph of $C(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/ad3fae3694c4df8269be9a54350aabf83368b3152ea69eb2b9d1ac76e702823f.png)\n\nApproximate the marginal cost $C'(x)$ at the point $x=3$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" 13194139535185 true] [17592186092032 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nC'(x) \\approx \\dfrac{C(x) - C(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=3$. Applying the above formula with $x=3$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\nC^{′}(3) \\mid \\approx (C(3) - C(3 - 2))/(2); = (C(3) - C(1))/(2); = \\frac{30 - 20}{2}; = \\frac{10}{2}; = 5\n$$\n\nSo the marginal cost is $\\[math]5$ per fan." 13194139535185 false] [17592186092032 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nC'(x) \\approx \\dfrac{C(x) - C(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=3.$ Applying the above formula with $x=3$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{C(3)-C(3-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{C(3)-C(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-20}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the marginal cost is $\\[math]5$ per fan." 13194139535185 true] [17592186092189 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336164 13194139535185 true] [17592186092189 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336198 13194139535185 true] [17592186092189 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336226 13194139535185 true] [17592186092189 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336233 13194139535185 true] [17592186092190 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336173 13194139535185 true] [17592186092190 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336177 13194139535185 true] [17592186092190 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336181 13194139535185 true] [17592186092190 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336184 13194139535185 true] [17592186092190 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336247 13194139535185 true] [17592186092337 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336153 13194139535185 true] [17592186092337 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336157 13194139535185 true] [17592186092337 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336160 13194139535185 true] [17592186092337 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336212 13194139535185 true] [17592186092337 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336219 13194139535185 true] [17592186092338 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336167 13194139535185 true] [17592186092338 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336170 13194139535185 true] [17592186092338 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336191 13194139535185 true] [17592186092338 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336205 13194139535185 true] [17592186092338 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336240 13194139535185 true] [17592186092677 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535185 true] [17592186092677 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/5773f1515892d8eb2cb77c700eab48f7cf44b0f91e9e157960798574e23ca93d.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" 13194139535185 true] [17592186092677 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/993/Source/Images/q-47547.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above.\nApproximate $g^{′}(x)$ at the point $x = 2$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h = 1$." 13194139535185 false] [17592186092677 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x)-g(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=2.$ . Applying the above formula with $x=2$ and $h=1$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{g(2)-g(2-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(2)-g(1)}{1} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336153 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336154 13194139535185 true] [17592186336153 1089 "q-343779" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336153 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535185 true] [17592186336153 1100 "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $0.7$ | $0.8$ | $0.9$ | $1.0$ | $1.1$ | $1.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $13$ | $15$ | $18$ | $20$ | $22$ | $25$ |\n\nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=1.2$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\,\\,{f}^{′}(1.2)≈$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336153 1113 #uuid "ca6e7459-ab08-5f57-bb26-c78c226031a0" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336153 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=1.2.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case, $h=0.1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1.2) & ≈\\frac{f(1.2)-f(1.2-0.1)}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1.2)-f(1.1)}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-22}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{0.1} \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336154 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336155 13194139535185 true] [17592186336154 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336156 13194139535185 true] [17592186336154 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535185 true] [17592186336154 1041 "field-1" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336154 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336156 13194139535185 true] [17592186336154 1110 #uuid "5d17bca4-dfa8-5637-80e2-f68f77f47757" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336155 1034 "3" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336155 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535185 true] [17592186336155 1111 #uuid "d29b44f2-49df-5106-8c47-3ed86fa2e77a" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336156 1034 "30" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336156 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535185 true] [17592186336156 1111 #uuid "0a14edba-101f-5f0f-8493-29f65b5ad90d" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336157 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336158 13194139535185 true] [17592186336157 1089 "q-343782" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336157 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535185 true] [17592186336157 1100 "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $-0.5$ | $-0.25$ | $0$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $10$ | $9$ | $8$ | $6$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=0.75$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\,\\,{f}^{′}(0.75)≈$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336157 1113 #uuid "464a1358-3b78-5c7e-9875-a51dcad5a536" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336157 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=0.75.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case, $h=0.25.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0.75) & ≈\\frac{f(0.75)-f(0.75-0.25)}{0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(0.75)-f(0.5)}{0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-5}{0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{0.25} \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336158 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336159 13194139535185 true] [17592186336158 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535185 true] [17592186336158 1041 "field-1" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336158 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336159 13194139535185 true] [17592186336158 1110 #uuid "5de84170-0bd4-5655-95ef-0e75e8996856" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336159 1034 "-8" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336159 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535185 true] [17592186336159 1111 #uuid "0893f434-ab7d-50b6-8c21-5cba86c52d17" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336160 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336161 13194139535185 true] [17592186336160 1089 "q-343788" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336160 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535185 true] [17592186336160 1100 "A distance function $s(t)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.02$ | $0.04$ | $0.06$ | $0.08$ | $0.10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $3$ | $5$ | $9$ | $13$ | $18$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity ${s}^{′}(t)$ at the point $t=0.06$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\,\\,{s}^{′}(0.06)≈$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336160 1113 #uuid "12b7a3fa-89bd-53d6-a916-5e09ca0e4446" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336160 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{s}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{s(t)-s(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $t=0.06.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case, $h=0.02.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(0.06) & ≈\\frac{s(0.06)-s(0.06-0.02)}{0.02} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(0.06)-s(0.04)}{0.02} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-5}{0.02} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{0.02} \\\\  & =200.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336161 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336162 13194139535185 true] [17592186336161 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336163 13194139535185 true] [17592186336161 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535185 true] [17592186336161 1041 "field-1" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336161 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336163 13194139535185 true] [17592186336161 1110 #uuid "f016f9dc-39b2-573f-b597-bceb05037c3f" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336162 1034 "4" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336162 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535185 true] [17592186336162 1111 #uuid "f532fdad-6d44-567e-8e55-a73ddffeee75" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336163 1034 "200" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336163 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535185 true] [17592186336163 1111 #uuid "7b0c0a7d-2feb-5df7-9804-99f3b8902915" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336164 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336165 13194139535185 true] [17592186336164 1089 "q-343816" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336164 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535185 true] [17592186336164 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/2c633342d5dc1ddb1244d492d81b6b9c00ffe33858398a72acb2a73467d93bb0.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nFind the approximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=2$ and $x=4.$*\n\n${g}^{′}(4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336164 1113 #uuid "aedcaddd-0808-5967-84ab-caaf86d93147" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336164 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x)-g(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=4.$ Applying the above formula with $x=4$ and $h=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{g(4)-g(4-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(4)-g(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336166 13194139535185 true] [17592186336165 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535185 true] [17592186336165 1041 "field-1" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336165 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336166 13194139535185 true] [17592186336165 1110 #uuid "29197f99-98d5-5393-abcb-5094b0a3fefa" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336166 1034 "2" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336166 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535185 true] [17592186336166 1111 #uuid "bf9cf71c-9be8-5841-8514-5c46185ff838" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336167 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336168 13194139535185 true] [17592186336167 1089 "q-343833" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336167 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535185 true] [17592186336167 1100 "The number of deer in a wildlife reserve, measured in hundreds, is given by the function $D(t)$, where $t$ is the time, in years, since the study began. The values of $D(t)$ for several values of $t$ are shown in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $D(t)$ | $5$ | $7$ | $10$ | $14$ | $19$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of the deer population when $t=4$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $t=4$ is\n{{field-1}} hundreds per year." 13194139535185 true] [17592186336167 1113 #uuid "d6b9060a-5e21-57bc-83ba-2a9d11c84c25" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336167 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{D}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{D(t)-D(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=4.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{D(4)-D(4-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{D(4)-D(3)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{19-14}{1} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $t=4$ is $5$ hundreds per year." 13194139535185 true] [17592186336168 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336169 13194139535185 true] [17592186336168 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535185 true] [17592186336168 1041 "field-1" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336168 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336169 13194139535185 true] [17592186336168 1110 #uuid "104a67c9-2c2b-5ec3-98ec-0cc137041014" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336169 1034 "5" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336169 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535185 true] [17592186336169 1111 #uuid "a91b1534-01ba-57e1-a8db-7e5965f8b0f4" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336170 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336171 13194139535185 true] [17592186336170 1089 "q-343841" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336170 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535185 true] [17592186336170 1100 "The battery charge of a delivery drone, measured in percent, is given by the function $B(t)$, where $t$ is the time, in hours, since the drone was fully charged. The values of $B(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B(t)$ | $80$ | $72$ | $55$ | $40$ | $35$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of the battery charge when $t=6$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $t=6$ is\n{{field-1}} percent per hour." 13194139535185 true] [17592186336170 1113 #uuid "ab708991-bfbd-575f-9834-64b4bea609d7" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336170 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{B}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{B(t)-B(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=6.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{B(6)-B(6-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{B(6)-B(4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-55}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $t=6$ is $-\\frac{15}{2}$ percent per hour." 13194139535185 true] [17592186336171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336172 13194139535185 true] [17592186336171 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535185 true] [17592186336171 1041 "field-1" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336171 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336172 13194139535185 true] [17592186336171 1110 #uuid "bda948df-57d9-507a-bbc7-6c1f9ecc1325" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336172 1034 "-7.5" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336172 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535185 true] [17592186336172 1111 #uuid "8e03e049-5f3e-5ff7-9758-94d9b6aacec1" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336173 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336174 13194139535185 true] [17592186336173 1089 "q-343858" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336173 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535185 true] [17592186336173 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/3a4526a6979a86aa924bddca54b05778e91bb67a1dcb6159c5436cc975b22668.png)\n\nA cyclist records the total distance traveled after $x$ minutes. The total distance traveled after $x$ minutes, in miles, is given by the function $D(x).$ The graph of $D(x)$ is shown above.  \n\nApproximate the rate of change when $x=45$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=15.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=30$ and $x=45.$*\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $x=45$ is\n{{field-1}} miles per minute." 13194139535185 true] [17592186336173 1113 #uuid "f719021c-ba29-5df8-bf19-5b8055797dc8" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336173 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{D}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{D(x)-D(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=45.$  Applying the above formula with $x=45$ and $h=15$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{′}(45) & ≈\\frac{D(45)-D(45-15)}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{D(45)-D(30)}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{11-6}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $x=45$ is $\\frac{1}{3}$ miles per minute." 13194139535185 true] [17592186336174 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336175 13194139535185 true] [17592186336174 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336176 13194139535185 true] [17592186336174 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535185 true] [17592186336174 1041 "field-1" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336174 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336175 13194139535185 true] [17592186336174 1110 #uuid "fc96f7f1-9038-5677-9efd-59393fa9de91" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336175 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336175 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535185 true] [17592186336175 1111 #uuid "ad5b6392-64ad-55fe-a988-3954b922cc3b" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336176 1034 "5" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336176 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535185 true] [17592186336176 1111 #uuid "2b043044-28cc-56a1-aa5c-5e5fbf2fb860" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336177 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336178 13194139535185 true] [17592186336177 1089 "q-343862" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336177 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535185 true] [17592186336177 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/9a5fa5cdd4734813d18af5b99e3869051f15e137f72e98ad5529c751e6f85d87.png)\n\nA model rocket’s height is recorded during a launch. The height after $x$ seconds, in meters, is given by the function $H(x).$ The graph of $H(x)$ is shown above.  \n\nApproximate the rate of change when $x=16$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=12.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=4$ and $x=16.$*\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $x=16$ is\n{{field-1}} meters per second." 13194139535185 true] [17592186336177 1113 #uuid "d0b47029-aa71-51b9-a42b-48c9fb06a339" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336177 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{H}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{H(x)-H(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=16.$  Applying the above formula with $x=16$ and $h=12$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}^{′}(16) & ≈\\frac{H(16)-H(16-12)}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{H(16)-H(4)}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-5}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $x=16$ is $\\frac{1}{6}$ meters per second." 13194139535185 true] [17592186336178 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336179 13194139535185 true] [17592186336178 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336180 13194139535185 true] [17592186336178 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535185 true] [17592186336178 1041 "field-1" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336178 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336180 13194139535185 true] [17592186336178 1110 #uuid "8304bd21-4ab9-51f9-bcd9-dee51fb9070a" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336179 1034 "2" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336179 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535185 true] [17592186336179 1111 #uuid "df9ac778-ac39-513d-95b0-966ef7c0c963" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336180 1034 "\\frac{1}{6}" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336180 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535185 true] [17592186336180 1111 #uuid "62094e1b-52dd-594e-aa6a-251ca1d942e1" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336181 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336182 13194139535185 true] [17592186336181 1089 "q-343869" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336181 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535185 true] [17592186336181 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/fd917228308b839be77110b07e32fa0154350c290920628400bd1f20a34160b5.png)\n\nA factory tracks the number of defective items remaining after a recall. The number of defective items after $x$ days, in hundreds, is given by the function $N(x).$ The graph of $N(x)$ is shown above.  \n\nApproximate the rate of change when $x=15$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=5.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=10$ and $x=15.$*\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $x=15$ is\n{{field-1}} hundred items per day." 13194139535185 true] [17592186336181 1113 #uuid "771ddffa-f1c8-5e73-8081-54fdf5cab8e9" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336181 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{N(x)-N(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=15.$  Applying the above formula with $x=15$ and $h=5$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{′}(15) & ≈\\frac{N(15)-N(15-5)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{N(15)-N(10)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{72-76}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $x=15$ is $-\\frac{4}{5}$ hundred items per day." 13194139535185 true] [17592186336182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336183 13194139535185 true] [17592186336182 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535185 true] [17592186336182 1041 "field-1" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336182 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336183 13194139535185 true] [17592186336182 1110 #uuid "f9ba6acf-8fbb-5bb3-8957-0d010726cc04" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336183 1034 "-0.8" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336183 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535185 true] [17592186336183 1111 #uuid "fa295e88-7dd7-548a-8b7f-d90bc23f98b3" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336184 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336185 13194139535185 true] [17592186336184 1089 "q-47357" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336184 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535185 true] [17592186336184 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/64e35c559b233a28c5913ffa37ad949653ad2b46a0e10db639626b8575b7a263.png)\n\nAn oil tank is leaking $q(t)$ liters of oil, where $t>0$ is the time in hours since the leak started.\n The graph of $q(t)$ is shown above.\n\nApproximate the rate at which the oil is leaking from the tank when $t=2$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336184 1113 #uuid "356c9356-b943-5cfb-849c-968981b6e053" 13194139535185 true] [17592186336184 1145 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t)-q(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=2.$ Applying the above formula with $t=2$ and $h=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{q(2)